            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Weekly Insight: Why Overseas Ad Reconciliation Has Become a Major Headache for Teams</h2>
<p>As we enter the Q4 sprint phase, many overseas teams are scaling up ad spend. However, three parallel tracks—recharges, spend, and invoices—often leave finance and marketing teams struggling with mismatched accounts.</p>
<h3>This Week's Key Reconciliation Challenges</h3>
<p><strong>The Timing Gap Between Recharge and Spend</strong></p>
<p>Multiple teams reported this week that充值 funds often take 24-48 hours to appear in accounts. Meta's "spend first, recharge later" model creates delays between actual balance and reported data. Google Ads and TikTok operate on different settlement cycles, making it difficult to establish consistent reconciliation granularity across the team.</p>
<p><strong>Lengthy Invoice Processes and Inconsistent Formats</strong></p>
<p>Invoices are critical for financial reconciliation. This week's observations reveal: Meta requires proactive monthly statement downloads, Google issues invoices per spend cycle, and TikTok invoice headers sometimes don't match the recharge account entity. These issues delay reimbursement and may affect tax compliance.</p>
<p><strong>Currency Fluctuations Causing Account Discrepancies</strong></p>
<p>Ad recharges involve multiple currencies (CNY, USD, HKD), while actual spend settles in the local currency of the target region. Exchange rate volatility creates gaps between recharge amounts and actual spend—small initially, but accumulating into blind spots over time.</p>
<h3>Three Key Steps for Unified Reconciliation</h3>
<p><strong>Step 1: Map Account Cycles, Build a Reconciliation Ledger</strong></p>
<p>Categorize all ad accounts by platform and record each account's recharge cycle, settlement cycle, and invoice timeline. Create a simple ledger tracking: account name, platform, recharge amount, spend amount, invoice status, and exchange rate. Update weekly on a fixed schedule.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2: Distinguish "Prepaid" vs. "Post-Spend" Models</strong></p>
<p>Prepaid accounts (e.g., certain TikTok accounts) should use recharge records as the baseline, with spend data as supplementary. Post-spend accounts (e.g., certain Google Ads accounts) should use spend reports as the baseline, treating recharges as balance top-ups. Clarifying this distinction eliminates "mismatched accounts" disputes.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3: Record Exchange Rate Differences Separately</strong></p>
<p>Set up a dedicated "foreign exchange gain/loss" or "rate adjustment" account in your financial system to capture exchange rate gaps between recharges and spend. This maintains data integrity for spend reporting while enabling clear audit trails.</p>
<h3>This Week's Recommendation: Build Processes, Don't Just Fix Problems After</h3>
<p>Reconciliation issues stem not from calculation errors but from misaligned processes. Many teams discover problems during month-end reconciliation when data has already accumulated, making troubleshooting significantly harder.</p>
<p>We recommend integrating ad financial reconciliation into daily operations rather than handling it all at month-end. Marketers share daily recharge screenshots, finance reconciles platform reports weekly, and invoices are filed immediately upon receipt. After 2-3 weeks, reconciliation efficiency improves noticeably, with financial risks effectively managed upstream.</p>
<p>Overseas ad reconciliation may seem complex, but once the three tracks—recharge, spend, and invoices—are straightened out, overall financial efficiency rises considerably. We hope this week's insights help your team transition from "firefighting reconciliation" to "process-based management."</p>
